::ENG4_U05_L01_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence correctly fixes this comma splice? Original: The survey was useful, it lacked enough detail.
{
=A. The survey was useful, but it lacked enough detail.#Correct. The comma and coordinating conjunction correctly show contrast.
~B. The survey was useful it lacked enough detail.#Incorrect. This is a run-on sentence.
~C. Because the survey was useful.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~D. The survey, was useful, it lacked detail.#Incorrect. This punctuation does not fix the comma splice.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source: Revision improved most when students focused on one purpose at a time. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. Revision improved when students tried to fix every problem at once.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
=C. Students revised more effectively when they concentrated on one clear goal at a time.#Correct. This preserves the source meaning.
~B. Revision was unrelated to purpose.#Incorrect. Purpose is central to the source.
~D. Students never revised their work.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which sentence uses precision correctly?
{
~A. Precision means using unclear words.#Incorrect. Precision means exactness.
~C. Precision means writing as vaguely as possible.#Incorrect. That is the opposite of precision.
=B. Precision improves when a writer replaces vague wording with exact language.#Correct. This accurately uses the term.
~D. Precision is the same as punctuation length.#Incorrect. Precision concerns exact meaning.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision best improves clarity? Original: The claim is strong it needs evidence.
{
~A. The claim is strong it needs evidence.#Incorrect. The run-on remains.
~B. The claim, is strong it needs evidence.#Incorrect. The comma after the subject is incorrect.
~C. Because the claim is strong it needs evidence.#Incorrect. This still lacks necessary punctuation and logic.
=D. The claim is strong, but it needs evidence.#Correct. This clearly shows the contrast.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence correctly uses a semicolon?
{
=A. The evidence is relevant; the explanation is incomplete.#Correct. A semicolon joins two complete related sentences.
~B. The evidence is relevant; because it supports the claim.#Incorrect. The second part is a dependent clause.
~C. The evidence; is relevant.#Incorrect. The semicolon separates a subject from its verb.
~D. Because the evidence; is relevant.#Incorrect. This breaks the clause.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source: Updates were useful when writers named the task, obstacle, and next step. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. Updates were useful only when writers avoided details.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
=C. Updates helped when writers clearly identified the work, problem, and next action.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~B. Updates were useful because they were long.#Incorrect. Length is not the source focus.
~D. Updates were never useful.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which phrase is most academically precise?
{
~A. stuff in writing#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. good grammar things#Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
=B. sentence-boundary control#Correct. This is precise academic language.
~D. words and stuff#Incorrect. This is vague.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] What should a writer do first when revising sentence structure?
{
~A. Add punctuation randomly.#Incorrect. Punctuation must match meaning.
~B. Delete all evidence.#Incorrect. That does not address sentence structure.
~C. Ignore the intended meaning.#Incorrect. Revision must preserve meaning.
=D. Identify the relationship between the ideas.#Correct. The relationship guides the convention choice.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence is a fragment?
{
=A. Because the report lacked evidence.#Correct. This dependent clause does not complete the thought.
~B. The report lacked evidence.#Incorrect. This is a complete sentence.
~C. The report lacked evidence, so the committee questioned it.#Incorrect. This is a complete sentence.
~D. The report lacked evidence; the committee questioned it.#Incorrect. This is a complete sentence.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Which paraphrase changes the source meaning? Source: The committee questioned the report because several claims lacked evidence.
{
~A. The committee doubted the report because some claims were unsupported.#Incorrect. This preserves the meaning.
=C. The committee trusted the report because every claim had evidence.#Correct. This reverses the source meaning.
~B. Some unsupported claims caused the committee to question the report.#Incorrect. This preserves the meaning.
~D. The report's unsupported claims made the committee doubt it.#Incorrect. This preserves the meaning.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence correctly uses a colon?
{
=B. The draft needs one change: the evidence must be explained.#Correct. The colon introduces the specific change.
~A. The draft needs: one change.#Incorrect. The colon separates a verb from its object.
~C. Because: the draft needs evidence.#Incorrect. The colon is incorrectly placed after a subordinating word.
~D. The draft: needs evidence.#Incorrect. This separates the subject from the verb.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision improves academic style?
{
~A. The evidence is kinda okay and stuff.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
~B. The evidence, like, supports things.#Incorrect. This is informal.
=D. The evidence supports the claim by identifying a specific delay in the process.#Correct. This is precise and academic.
~C. The evidence is good because good.#Incorrect. This is circular.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence contains a run-on?
{
=A. The claim is clear the evidence is relevant.#Correct. Two complete thoughts are joined without punctuation.
~B. The claim is clear, and the evidence is relevant.#Incorrect. This is correctly punctuated.
~C. The claim is clear; the evidence is relevant.#Incorrect. This is correctly punctuated.
~D. The claim is clear because the evidence is relevant.#Incorrect. This is a complete sentence.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] In writing, credibility is strengthened by:
{
~A. unclear punctuation and unsupported claims.#Incorrect. These weaken credibility.
=C. clear conventions, precise vocabulary, and accurate evidence.#Correct. These support reader trust.
~B. random commas and vague wording.#Incorrect. These weaken clarity.
~D. fragments in every sentence.#Incorrect. Fragments usually weaken formal writing.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision preserves meaning while improving punctuation?
{
~A. The report included data it was persuasive.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
~C. The report included data, it was persuasive.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
=B. The report included data, so it was persuasive.#Correct. This uses punctuation and a conjunction to show cause and effect.
~D. Because the report included data.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence uses subordination correctly?
{
~A. Although the report used current data.#Incorrect. This fragment begins a dependent clause but never completes it.
=D. Although the report used current data, it did not explain the method.#Correct. The dependent clause and main clause form a complete sentence.
~C. The report although used current data.#Incorrect. This is not standard sentence structure.
~B. Although, the report, current data.#Incorrect. This is fragmented.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Why do conventions matter in paraphrase?
{
=A. Unclear punctuation can change or obscure the source meaning.#Correct. Paraphrase must preserve meaning clearly.
~B. Punctuation lets writers change the source however they want.#Incorrect. Paraphrase must remain accurate.
~C. Conventions are unrelated to meaning.#Incorrect. Conventions affect meaning.
~D. Paraphrase should avoid sentence structure.#Incorrect. Paraphrase requires clear sentences.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which punctuation best joins two complete, closely related sentences without a conjunction?
{
~A. A random apostrophe.#Incorrect. Apostrophes do not join sentences.
=C. A semicolon.#Correct. A semicolon can join two complete related sentences.
~B. No punctuation.#Incorrect. That creates a run-on.
~D. A comma alone.#Incorrect. A comma alone creates a comma splice.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which question best guides revision?
{
~A. Can I add commas anywhere?#Incorrect. Revision should be purposeful.
=B. Does the punctuation show the relationship between ideas?#Correct. This checks clarity and logic.
~C. Can I make the sentence longer for no reason?#Incorrect. Length is not the goal.
~D. Can I remove the main idea?#Incorrect. Revision should strengthen meaning.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which sentence uses "coordination" correctly?
{
~A. Coordination means making one idea dependent on another.#Incorrect. That describes subordination.
=D. Coordination connects equal ideas in a sentence.#Correct. This accurately defines coordination.
~C. Coordination means deleting punctuation.#Incorrect. Coordination often uses punctuation.
~B. Coordination means changing source meaning.#Incorrect. Coordination concerns sentence relationship.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence is correctly punctuated?
{
=A. The writer revised the claim, and the evidence became easier to follow.#Correct. The comma and conjunction correctly join two complete ideas.
~B. The writer revised the claim, the evidence became easier to follow.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~C. The writer revised the claim the evidence became easier to follow.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
~D. Because the writer revised the claim.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision best improves a vague sentence?
{
~A. The writing has things.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. The writing has better stuff.#Incorrect. This remains vague.
=C. The revision clarifies the claim by naming the specific policy being evaluated.#Correct. This is specific and clear.
~D. The revision is good because it is good.#Incorrect. This is circular.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source: Students improved drafts when they focused on one revision goal. Which paraphrase is inaccurate?
{
~A. Drafts improved when students revised for one clear purpose.#Incorrect. This is accurate.
~C. Students strengthened drafts by concentrating on one revision goal.#Incorrect. This is accurate.
=B. Students improved drafts by ignoring revision goals.#Correct. This contradicts the source.
~D. One focused revision goal helped students improve drafts.#Incorrect. This is accurate.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence correctly uses a dependent clause?
{
~A. Although the paragraph was clear.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
=D. Because the evidence was specific, the paragraph became more persuasive.#Correct. The dependent clause is joined to a complete main clause.
~C. Since the evidence.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
~B. While the claim.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which statement best summarizes the lesson focus?
{
=A. Standard conventions help advanced writers preserve meaning, show logic, and revise for clarity.#Correct. This captures the lesson's focus.
~B. Conventions are unrelated to meaning.#Incorrect. They shape meaning.
~C. Punctuation should be random.#Incorrect. Punctuation should be purposeful.
~D. Revision should ignore sentence clarity.#Incorrect. Revision should improve clarity.
}
